<Guide
  lang="en"
  title="What is SEO — in plain words? | PISMA"
  description="SEO means being found on Google by the people who need you, without paying for ads. See what matters for your business and what changes with AI."
  slug="seo-in-plain-words"
  question="What is SEO — in plain words?"
  sub="For business owners who keep hearing “SEO” and finally want to understand what it is — no tech talk."
  answer="SEO (Search Engine Optimization) means shaping your website and your profile so that Google shows you when someone searches for what you offer — without paying for advertising. Google recommends the pages that answer the user’s question best and most reliably; SEO’s job is to make you that answer. The same now goes for AI tools like ChatGPT, which recommend businesses using similar logic."
  updated="2026-07-28"
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>What does SEO mean, and how is it different from advertising?</h2>
  <p>
    SEO stands for Search Engine Optimization. In plain words: setting up your presence on the
    internet so that when someone searches “electrician near me” or “where to eat pizza”, you are
    the one who shows up.
  </p>
  <p>
    The difference from advertising is simple: with ads you pay for every appearance, and the
    moment you stop paying, you disappear. SEO is work that builds up — pages, profiles,
    reviews — and keeps bringing people in without a meter running. It takes longer than
    advertising, but what you build stays yours.
  </p>

  <h2>How does Google “think”?</h2>
  <p>
    Google is, at its core, a machine that answers questions. Every search is a question — even
    when there is no question mark — and Google wants to show first the page that gives the most
    useful and trustworthy answer.
  </p>
  <p>To decide that, it looks at things like:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What your page answers</strong> — does it speak clearly to what the user is looking for, or is it vague?</li>
    <li><strong>How easy it is to read</strong> — does it load fast, does it work properly on mobile?</li>
    <li><strong>Whether others trust you</strong> — reviews, mentions, an up-to-date profile.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You don’t need to “trick” Google — that doesn’t work anymore anyway. You need to genuinely
    be the best answer for the customers in your area.
  </p>

  <h2>What actually matters for your business?</h2>
  <p>
    You don’t need everything the big companies do. For your business, four things are
    essential:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Pages that answer questions.</strong> One page for each key question your customers
      ask — what you offer, where, how you work. Not a shop-window site that only says
      “welcome”.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Speed and mobile.</strong> Most customers look you up on their phone. If the site
      is slow or hard to read there, they leave before they even see what you do.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Google profile and reviews.</strong> For local searches, your
      <a href="/en/guides/google-maps-profile/">profile on Google and Maps</a> is often the
      first thing a customer sees — before the website itself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Consistent details.</strong> Same name, same address, same phone number
      everywhere — on the site, on the Google profile, on social. Search engines get confused by
      mismatches, and customers even more so.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What changes with AI — what are AEO and GEO?</h2>
  <p>
    The new chapter is that customers no longer search only on Google — they also ask AI tools
    like ChatGPT: “where can I get my brakes done in the southern suburbs?”. And AI does not return a long
    list of links — it gives one answer, with a few specific business recommendations.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is where two new terms come in: AEO (Answer Engine Optimization) and GEO (Generative
    Engine Optimization) — in other words, being inside the answers AI gives. The good news:
    whatever you do right for SEO helps here too, because AI learns from the same sources — your
    website, your profile, your reviews. We wrote a separate guide on
    <a href="/en/guides/get-recommended-by-chatgpt/">how to get recommended by ChatGPT</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What can you do on your own, today?</h2>
  <p>Before you pay for anything, do these — they are free and only take a little time:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Search Google for your service and your area, the way a customer would type it. Do you show up? Where?</li>
    <li>Ask ChatGPT the same thing. Does it mention you? Who does it recommend?</li>
    <li>Fill in your Google profile completely: hours, photos, services, description.</li>
    <li>Check that your name, address and phone number are identical on your site, Google and social.</li>
    <li>Open your website on a phone. Is it easy to read? Can you find the phone number in one move?</li>
    <li>Write one page or post that answers your customers’ most frequent question.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How does PISMA help?</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Visibility on Google & AI</strong> service covers exactly this: SEO, AEO and GEO
    together. The initial setup costs {eurEn(aiSetup.price)} one-off and monthly management is
    {eurEn(aiMonthly.price)} per month, with a monthly report on what was done and what was
    measured — no commitment, no contract. We measure where you stand before we start and
    re-measure at 3 months, so you can judge with numbers, not promises. If you first just want
    to see where you are, book the free <a href="/en/free-assessment/">PISMA Check</a>:
    {ELEGXOS.minutes} minutes, and you leave with a written picture of your Google, AI and
    day-to-day operations.
  </p>
</Guide>
